# Pack Management

This section covers the practical toolchain around building, versioning, exporting, testing, and publishing packs.

## Platforms

- [CurseForge](/wiki/modpack-management/curseforge)
- [Modrinth](/wiki/modpack-management/modrinth)
- [Project management](/wiki/modpack-management/project-management)

## Tools

- [packwand](/wiki/modpack-management/packwand)
- [packwiz](/wiki/modpack-management/packwiz)
- [packwiz components](/wiki/modpack-management/packwiz/components)
- [Pakku](https://juraj-hrivnak.github.io/Pakku/home.html)

## Choosing a tool

| Need | Best fit | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Maintain one or more packs as metadata in Git | [packwand](/wiki/modpack-management/packwand) | Adds higher-level workflows on top of packwiz, including workspace operations, publishing, diffing, validation, and a local GUI/API |
| Maintain a single pack with a smaller CLI surface | [packwiz](/wiki/modpack-management/packwiz) | Mature TOML-based pack format with straightforward install/export workflows |
| Distribute updates to players or servers | [packwiz components](/wiki/modpack-management/packwiz/components) | The bootstrap and installer handle launch-time updates and optional mods |
| Produce a hosted `.mrpack` from an existing manifest | [Pakku](https://juraj-hrivnak.github.io/Pakku/home.html) | Useful when you want an external exporter around the packwiz-style ecosystem |

## Recommended workflows

1. Author the pack as metadata, not as a folder of downloaded JARs.
2. Keep the pack in Git so recipe, config, and dependency changes are reviewable.
3. Use `packwand` when you need repository-aware workflows such as workspace sync, bulk updates, publishing plans, or diagnostics.
4. Use `packwiz` directly when you want the smaller original toolchain and you do not need the packwand-specific repository automation.
5. Test the player install path with the bootstrap and installer before you publish a release.

## Mental model

- `packwiz` defines the core manifest format and the classic CLI workflow.
- `packwand` builds on that format and adds repository automation, publishing, diagnostics, and multi-pack support.
- `packwiz-installer` and the [bootstrap](/wiki/modpack-management/packwiz/components/bootstrap) are runtime delivery tools for players and servers.
- Hosting targets such as CurseForge and Modrinth are output formats and distribution channels, not your source of truth.